\n\
Let me also think ahead — their Suki Kyoshi Warrior: power = number of creatures they control. They currently control: Bear, Forest-creature, Earth King, Toph, Suki = 5 creatures. If I kill the Bear... I'm not killing it. Next turn they attack with Suki, make an Ally token → 6 creatures → Suki 6/4 when attacking... wait, the token is created tapped and attacking, so during combat they have 6 creatures, Suki is 6/4.\n\
\n\
I'll deal with that later. Maybe Barrels can shoot Suki for 5 (4 toughness) — yes, Barrels deals 5 to Suki, killing it. That costs 5 mana + tap + sac. I have 7 lands, so feasible.\n\
\n\
OK here's my plan: Block Bear with Inspector. Then next turn: Merchants attacks, sac Clue, draw, Merchants 6/5. See what happens. Maybe also cast Freedom Fighters.\n\
\n\
Actually wait, should I consider blocking with BOTH Inspector and Merchants? Bear 4/4 blocked by both: Bear assigns 4 damage — must assign lethal to one before the next. Kills Inspector (1 damage) then 3 to Merchants. Merchants 5/4 takes 3, survives. Both blockers deal damage: 5+1=6 to Bear, Bear dies. I lose Inspector (take 1, get Clue), Bear dies, Merchants survives as 5/4 with 3 damage marked (heals at cleanup).\n\
\n\
That's actually good! Bear dies, Merchants survives, I get a Clue. Cost: Inspector + 1 life.\n\
\n\
Compare to blocking with Inspector alone: Bear survives, I keep... no wait, Inspector dies in both cases. \n\
\n\
Double-block: Bear dies, Merchants survives (takes 3, has 4 toughness, lives), Inspector dies (I take 1, get Clue). I go to 12.\n\
Single-block Inspector: Bear survives (takes 1), Inspector dies (I take 1, get Clue). I go to 12.\n\
\n\
Same life, same Inspector loss, same Clue. Difference: double-block kills the Bear but Merchants takes 3 damage (irrelevant, heals). \n\
\n\
Wait, does Bear assign damage correctly? Bear is 4/4. Blockers: Inspector (1 toughness) and Merchants (4 toughness). Attacker orders blockers. Bear must assign lethal damage to first blocker before assigning to next. If opponent orders Inspector first: 1 damage to Inspector (lethal), 3 to Merchants. Merchants survives with 3 damage marked. Bear takes 6 damage (5 from Merchants + 1 from Inspector), dies.\n\
\n\
If opponent orders Merchants first: must assign lethal (4) to Merchants before Inspector. 4 damage to Merchants = lethal (4 toughness). Then 0 to Inspector. Inspector survives! Merchants dies. Bear takes 5 damage from Merchants, dies. \n\
\n\
Oh no — the ATTACKER chooses damage assignment order. So opponent would order Merchants first, kill Merchants, and Inspector survives. Bear still dies (takes 5 from Merchants).\n\
\n\
So double-block outcomes (opponent chooses):\n\
- Option A (they order Merchants first): Merchants dies, Bear dies, Inspector survives. I keep Inspector but lose Merchants.\n\
- Option B (they order Inspector first): Inspector dies, Merchants survives with 3 damage, Bear dies, I get Clue, take 1.\n\
\n\
Opponent picks A, obviously — killing Merchants is huge for them. Trade their token for my Merchants? Yes please.\n\
\n\
So double-blocking is bad: I lose Merchants (my best creature) for a Bear token. Even though Bear dies, that's a bad trade.\n\
\n\
Single block with Inspector: Bear survives, Inspector dies, I get Clue, take 1, go to 12.\n\
\n\
Hmm, but is Bear surviving bad? Bear is a 4/4. Next turn they attack with Bear + Suki + others. \n\
\n\
Alternatively, no block: take 4, go to 9. Keep Inspector. Next turn attack with Merchants, sac Inspector (take 1, go to 8), draw, Merchants 6/5.\n\
\n\
Let me count the race more carefully.\n\
\n\
My turn 9 (after their turn 8 combat): I have Merchants 5/4, Inspector (if not blocked) or Clue (if blocked), 7 lands, Barrels, Freedom Fighters in hand. Opponent at 7.\n\
\n\
Turn 9 attack: Merchants (sac Inspector or Clue → 6/5), plus cast Freedom Fighters precombat (2/1 haste + 1/1 Ally token). Attack with Merchants 6/5, Freedom Fighters 2/1, Ally 1/1, and Inspector 1/1 menace if still alive (if I didn't block and didn't sac it... but I want to sac it for the draw).\n\
\n\
Opponent's blockers on their turn 9... wait no, my turn 9 attack is blocked by their untapped creatures: Bear 4/4, Earth King 2/2, Toph 2/3, Suki 5/4, Forest 2/2 (earthbent land, can block). That's a lot of blockers. They easily block everything and kill my stuff. Merchants 6/5 gets double-blocked by Bear + Earth King: kills both, dies. Or Bear + Toph: 6 damage, kills both, Merchants takes 6, dies.\n\
\n\
Hmm, attacking into that board is rough. But I have Barrels: 5 mana, tap, sac, deal 5 to a creature. I can kill Suki or Bear with it.\n\
\n\
Let me think about my actual win condition. Maybe I'm not winning by combat soon; maybe I grind with Merchants drawing cards.\n\
\n\
Actually, let's reconsider: opponent at 7 life. I have on board: Merchants 5/4, Inspector 1/1, Barrels. Hand: Freedom Fighters. \n\
\n\
What if next turn: cast Freedom Fighters (2/1 + 1/1 Ally). Attack with everything: Merchants 5/4 (sac Clue/Inspector → 6/5), FF 2/1, Ally 1/1, maybe Inspector 1/1 menace.\n\
\n\
Total power if Inspector alive and not sacced: 6+2+1+1 = 10, but they have 5 blockers with total toughness... they can block everything. Menace on Inspector means they need 2 blockers for it.\n\
\n\
They have: Bear 4/4, Earth King 2/2, Toph 2/3, Suki 5/4, Forest 2/2 = 5 blockers. My attackers: Merchants 6/5, FF 2/1, Ally 1/1, Inspector 1/1 menace = 4 attackers. Inspector needs 2 blockers. They can block all 4 attackers (using 5 blockers, 2 on Inspector). No damage gets through. Bad.\n\
\n\
Alternatively, use Barrels to kill a blocker first. Barrels: {5}, {T}, sac: 5 damage to target creature. Kill Suki (their anthem... no, Suki Kyoshi is the 5/4 attacker). Kill Bear 4/4. Then attack: they have 4 blockers (Earth King, Toph, Suki, Forest) for my 4 attackers, Inspector needs 2 → they block everything. Still no damage.\n\
\n\
Hmm. This board stall favors... whom? They have Suki Kyoshi growing. Each turn Suki attacks makes a token, increasing creature count, making Suki bigger. That's a problem. Also Earth King: when their power-4+ creatures attack, they ramp lands.\n\
\n\
I think I need to be aggressive and use Barrels + combat to push damage, or use Day of Black Sun... already used. \n\
\n\
Let me think about my deck: I have 25 cards left. What removal/threats remain? Unknown, but I've seen my decklist partially: Callous Inspector x2, Hog-Monkey, Day of Black Sun, Swampsnare Trap, Barrels, Beetle-Headed Merchants, Treetop Freedom Fighters. There must be more cards.\n\
\n\
OK, decision now: block with Inspector alone seems right. I go to 12, get a Clue, keep Merchants intact. The Bear stays, but I can deal with it: next turn I can use Barrels to shoot the Bear (5 damage kills 4/4) or Suki.\n\
\n\
Actually wait — maybe I shouldn't block at all and keep Inspector? Let me compare life totals: block → 12. No block → 9. In the no-block line, I keep Inspector which is a menace attacker and sac fodder. But saccing Inspector pings me for 1 anyway (8). Blocking gives me a Clue which is free sac fodder without the ping. And 12 > 9.\n\
\n\
The value of Inspector as a menace attacker: opponent has 5 creatures, plenty of double-blockers, so Inspector isn't getting through anyway. Its main value is sac fodder, which the Clue replaces. So blocking with Inspector is strictly better: 12 life + Clue vs 9 life + Inspector. Clue ≈ Inspector for sac purposes (Clue better: no ping, can also crack for 2 mana if needed). And 3 life difference.\n\
\n\
Block with Inspector. \n\
\n\
Rationale: Chump the Bear with Inspector — the 1 self-damage is cheaper than taking 4, and the Clue token replaces Inspector as perfect sacrifice fodder for Merchants' attack trigger next turn (free draw, no life loss). I won't double-block with Merchants because the opponent would order damage to kill Merchants,trading my best creature for a mere token.\n\
\n\
Let me do it.",
